---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/list-growth';
const title = 'SMS List Growth Without Consent Debt';
const desc = description(site, 'guides/list-growth');


---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/guides" class="link">← All SMS guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-8">
      <h2>Grow a reachable audience, not a phone-number counter</h2>
      <p>This guide is for acquisition managers deciding which signup surfaces deserve traffic. A submitted phone number, a recorded SMS subscription and a customer who understands the offer are different stages. Evaluate each surface on eligible subscribers and downstream quality rather than popup submissions alone. Do not use a shipping phone field as a substitute for a marketing choice.</p>
      <h2>Inventory each acquisition surface</h2>
      <p>Record checkout, onsite form, keyword, QR code and campaign landing page separately. Give each version a source ID, disclosure version, launch date, audience region, incentive and destination workflow. Archive the complete experience including the ad or sign that introduces a keyword. A form screenshot without the surrounding promise cannot explain what the customer expected. Retire unused forms so old offers do not keep enrolling people.</p>
      <h2>Understand what checkout actually saves</h2>
      <p>Shopify documents SMS checkout collection and says the marketing preference is saved when the customer submits checkout. A typed number in an abandoned checkout is therefore not proof that a subscription was saved. Reconcile the actual recorded subscription state before welcome enrollment. Keep an incomplete submission distinct from a rejection so a reporting team does not inflate either subscribers or opt-outs.</p>
      <h2>Make the offer match the welcome</h2>
      <p>Explain the identity and purpose of the messages at the capture point, with the applicable disclosures reviewed for the target jurisdiction. If the invitation promises stock alerts, do not silently enroll that person into every sale broadcast. Keep email and SMS choices separately attributable. A discount should have visible exclusions and redemption conditions; make the welcome link land on the promised offer rather than a generic homepage.</p>
      <h2>Use source cohorts to diagnose growth quality</h2>
      <p>Track exposure, completed subscriptions, first-message delivery outcomes, early opt-outs, offer redemption and later orders by source and version. Use consistent observation windows so a new cohort is not compared with a mature one. Distinguish duplicate submissions from net new eligible subscribers, and report removals separately. A source with fewer subscribers may still be useful if it attracts people whose requested alerts the store can fulfill.</p>
      <h2>A hypothetical denominator example</h2>
      <p>Suppose a proposed form experiment records 1,000 eligible sessions, 80 submissions and 60 new eligible SMS subscribers after duplicates and incomplete records are removed. Submission rate is 8%; eligible subscriber rate is 6%. If acquisition and incentive costs total $120, cost per new eligible subscriber is $2. These invented inputs illustrate accounting only; they predict neither conversion nor a market benchmark. Include messaging charges separately when evaluating the resulting cohort.</p>
      <h2>Handle existing lists without manufacturing permission</h2>
      <p>For a migration, preserve the original source, disclosure and subscription history. A purchased list or an email-only export is not a fresh SMS acquisition source. Quarantine records with missing permission evidence instead of enrolling them into a welcome flow to see who complains. Twilio policy requires sender- and subject-specific consent for its services; this supplier policy does not settle every local legal requirement.</p>
      <h2>Proposed capture acceptance cases</h2>
      <p>Before paid traffic, inspect mobile and desktop versions, decline the offer, submit an incomplete checkout, submit twice, and use an already unsubscribed fixture. Verify the recorded state, source version, discount eligibility and resulting workflow enrollment. Check that a changed form does not lose its disclosure history. These are proposed checks for your configuration; no conversion test or merchant result is claimed here.</p>
      <h2>Choose acquisition tooling around the record it leaves</h2>
      <p>Ask an app supplier to export one complete signup record and show how edits to forms are versioned. Evaluate whether checkout, onsite and keyword subscriptions remain distinguishable after synchronization and migration. Sequenzy advertises forms and a paid-plan SMS/MMS beta with US/Canada toll-free scope. Evaluate its exact capture-to-Shopify behavior before choosing it for this job; the supplier feature list alone does not validate your signup path.</p>
      <h2>Sources and related decisions</h2>
      <p>Supplier documentation checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy current product scope</a>. <a href="https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/messaging-policy">Twilio messaging policy</a>. <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/customer-contact-information">Shopify contact collection</a>. Product claims are scoped to these supplier statements; implementation and jurisdiction-specific requirements still need review.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/consent">Consent</a> · <a href="/guides/segmentation">Segmentation</a> · <a href="/apps">App catalog</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
